⊕meta⊕
name = "chain-invoke-root"
description = "Invoke-chain root. Logs a marker, then explicitly invokes chain-invoke-leaf by name. Proves that an invoke (naming) crosses the enabled gate: works even when the leaf is registered enabled=0. Fire via: skyway run chain-invoke-root"
trigger.manual = true
output_style = "terse"
⊕⊕

※start※
Bash root node. Appends a start marker to the shared chain-test log, then hands off to the leaf.
※※

§start§
bash = "echo \"invoke-root:start $(date -u +%s)\" >> /tmp/skyway-chain-test.log"
§§

※leaf※
Invoke node. Calls chain-invoke-leaf by name with an explicit marker var. This is composition by naming, so it is always allowed regardless of the leaf's enabled flag.
※※

§leaf§
depends_on = ["start"]
invoke.target = "chain-invoke-leaf"
invoke.vars = {"marker": "from-root"}
§§

※※
chain-invoke-root: invoke-chain proof

Pure bash, zero cost, zero credentials. Run it and inspect /tmp/skyway-chain-test.log:
two lines appear, invoke-root:start then invoke-leaf:land marker=from-root.

The point: a cache-resolved workflow explicitly invoking another cache-resolved
workflow runs even when both sit at enabled=0. Naming crosses the gate.
※※
